# Summary

=== layout: one-panel

# Delivery Is Part of the Mechanism

@body

{table-style=comparison}
| Platform | Useful Feature | Important Constraint |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Adenovirus | Strong expression | Immune response and redosing |
| AAV | Durable expression in some tissues | Small payload and capsid immunity |
| Retroviral vector | Stable integration | Insertion site and cell selection |
| Lipid particle | Transient RNA delivery | Tissue targeting and endosomal escape |
| Engineered cells | Test cells before infusion | Manufacturing, persistence, and toxicity |

=== layout: one-panel

# Choose the Change Before the Tool

@body

- Add a useful gene: deliver an expression cassette to the right cells.
- Reduce a harmful message: match an RNA medicine to its RNA target.
- Change DNA: choose a nuclease, base editor, or prime editor that can make the needed edit.
- Change gene activity: target regulatory machinery without changing the DNA letters.
- Replace or redirect cells: establish their identity, function, and behavior after delivery.

=== layout: bookend

# Key Takeaways

@body

- Delivery is part of the mechanism: the same payload can succeed or fail in different tissues and formats.
- Genome editing adds the challenge of permanent, cell-specific changes and unintended outcomes.
- Cell and cancer therapies work only when their biological target, evidence, and limits are made explicit.

